const legacyScenarios=[
  {id:"s1",question:"The interviewer asks about a gap in employment.",choices:["Avoid the question and immediately change the subject.","Give a brief honest explanation, state what you did during the period, and explain why you are ready now.","Invent a role so the timeline looks stronger.","Apologize repeatedly for the gap."],correct:1,feedback:[
    {heading:"Avoiding the gap creates more concern.",impact:"Changing the subject may make the interviewer think you are hiding something or cannot discuss difficult topics directly.",coaching:"Answer briefly, protect private details, and move the conversation toward what you did and why you are ready now.",sample:"I took time away from work to address a personal matter that is now resolved. During that period I kept my skills current, and I am ready to return to reliable, full-time work."},
    {heading:"Strong choice: honest, brief, and forward-looking.",impact:"This shows integrity, self-awareness, and current readiness without turning the interview into a long personal explanation.",coaching:"Keep the explanation under about 30 seconds and finish by connecting your readiness to this position.",sample:"I stepped away from work for a family responsibility that has been resolved. I used the time productively, and I am now prepared to bring my experience and reliability to this role."},
    {heading:"Inventing experience can end the hiring process.",impact:"A false role can be uncovered during reference or background checks and raises a serious integrity concern.",coaching:"Never create employment that did not happen. Use truthful volunteer work, training, caregiving, recovery, or skill-building when relevant.",sample:"I was not formally employed during that period. I was handling personal responsibilities and working on my readiness to return. I can explain the relevant skills I maintained and why I am ready now."},
    {heading:"Repeated apologies weaken an otherwise manageable answer.",impact:"Over-apologizing can make a normal employment gap sound like a continuing problem and can signal low confidence.",coaching:"Acknowledge the gap once, state the useful facts, and finish with present readiness—not shame.",sample:"There is a gap in my employment history because I needed to address a personal matter. That situation is stable now, and I am focused on returning to work and contributing consistently."}
  ]},
  {id:"s2",question:"You need several seconds to organize a difficult answer.",choices:["Begin speaking immediately and use filler words until the answer appears.","Say, “That is a good question. Let me think for a moment,” pause, and then answer.","Tell the interviewer the question is unfair.","Give the shortest possible answer and look away."],correct:1,feedback:[
    {heading:"Filling the silence makes the answer harder to follow.",impact:"A stream of “um,” “like,” and unfinished thoughts may sound unprepared even when you know the subject.",coaching:"Use one professional sentence to claim thinking time, pause, and organize the answer into situation, action, and result.",sample:"That is a good question. Let me think of the strongest example for a moment. [Pause.] In my last position, the situation was…"},
    {heading:"Strong choice: a deliberate pause shows control.",impact:"This communicates thoughtfulness and confidence. The interviewer can see that you are selecting a relevant example instead of panicking.",coaching:"Keep the pause comfortable—usually three to five seconds—then answer directly with a specific example.",sample:"Let me take a moment to organize that. [Pause.] A good example would be when I had to resolve…"},
    {heading:"Calling the question unfair sounds defensive.",impact:"The interviewer may conclude that you resist pressure, feedback, or reasonable follow-up questions.",coaching:"If the question is unclear, request clarification without accusing the interviewer.",sample:"I want to make sure I answer what you are asking. Would you like an example from my most recent job or from any point in my work history?"},
    {heading:"A rushed answer and looking away can appear evasive.",impact:"It may suggest discomfort, lack of interest, or that you have not fully considered the question.",coaching:"Stay visually engaged, pause, and give one complete point instead of escaping with the shortest answer possible.",sample:"I would like a moment to think about that. [Pause.] The main lesson I learned was…, and here is how I applied it afterward."}
  ]},
  {id:"s3",question:"You do not have every qualification listed in the posting.",choices:["Claim that you have experience you do not have.","Focus only on the missing qualification.","Acknowledge the gap, connect related experience, and explain a realistic learning plan.","Tell the employer the requirement is unnecessary."],correct:2,feedback:[
    {heading:"Claiming experience you do not have is a serious risk.",impact:"The employer may test the skill, check references, or discover the claim after hiring. That turns a trainable gap into an integrity problem.",coaching:"State the gap honestly, then bridge to related experience and evidence that you can learn the missing skill.",sample:"I have not used that exact system yet. I have used similar software to complete comparable tasks, and I am prepared to complete training and practice before my start date."},
    {heading:"Focusing only on the gap undersells you.",impact:"The interviewer hears what you cannot do but receives no reason to believe you can succeed.",coaching:"Mention the missing qualification once, then spend most of the answer on transferable skills, results, and your learning plan.",sample:"I do not yet have direct experience with that tool, but I have three years of related technical work, including troubleshooting and documentation. I would use the same learning process to become productive quickly."},
    {heading:"Strong choice: honest, relevant, and coachable.",impact:"This protects your credibility while showing that the missing qualification may be manageable.",coaching:"Make the answer convincing by naming one related skill, one past learning success, and one concrete step you will take.",sample:"I have not worked with that exact platform. I have used two comparable systems and learned the second one during my first month on the job. I would complete the available training and practice in a test environment so I can contribute quickly."},
    {heading:"Dismissing the requirement challenges the employer’s judgment.",impact:"It can sound arrogant and suggests that you may resist standards, instructions, or coaching.",coaching:"Respect the requirement even if you do not fully understand it. Ask how the skill is used and explain how you would close the gap.",sample:"I noticed that requirement and would like to understand how often the team uses it. I have related experience, and I am willing to complete the training needed to meet your standard."}
  ]},
  {id:"s4",question:"The interviewer asks, “Why do you want this job?”",choices:["Give only a list of duties from the posting.","Say that you will take any available job.","Connect the employer’s needs with your experience, interests, and the contribution you want to make.","Describe only what you want the employer to give you."],correct:2,feedback:[
    {heading:"Repeating the posting does not explain your motivation.",impact:"It proves you read the duties, but it does not show why this organization, team, or work matters to you.",coaching:"Select one employer need, connect it to your experience, and explain the contribution you want to make.",sample:"I am interested because this role combines customer service with problem-solving. Those are strengths I used successfully in my last position, and I would like to apply them on a team that values reliable service."},
    {heading:"“I will take any job” signals desperation, not fit.",impact:"The employer may doubt that you understand the role or will remain after a different opportunity appears.",coaching:"You can be honest about needing work while still naming real reasons this particular job fits your skills and goals.",sample:"I am actively looking for work, and this position stands out because it uses my experience in scheduling, customer communication, and solving day-to-day problems."},
    {heading:"Strong choice: it connects motivation with employer value.",impact:"The answer shows that you understand the job, have relevant evidence, and are interested in contributing—not only receiving a paycheck.",coaching:"Make it specific by naming the employer, one important need, and one example of relevant success.",sample:"I want this position because your team needs someone who can keep customers informed and solve problems calmly. In my last role, I handled high-volume requests while maintaining accurate records, and I would bring that same reliability here."},
    {heading:"Discussing only what you will receive sounds one-sided.",impact:"Pay, benefits, and advancement matter, but leading only with them can make the employer question your interest in the work itself.",coaching:"Balance what attracts you with what you can contribute to the employer.",sample:"The stability and growth opportunity are attractive, and I am also interested because my experience in inventory accuracy and team coordination matches what this department needs."}
  ]},
  {id:"s5",question:"The interviewer seems formal and reserved.",choices:["Force jokes into the conversation.","Match the professional tone, remain warm, and let rapport build naturally.","Assume they dislike you and become withdrawn.","Increase your volume and energy until they respond."],correct:1,feedback:[
    {heading:"Forced humor ignores the interviewer’s communication style.",impact:"It can feel distracting, uncomfortable, or like you are trying too hard to win approval.",coaching:"Remain friendly without performing. Let humor appear naturally only if the interviewer’s response supports it.",sample:"Good morning. Thank you for meeting with me. I have been looking forward to learning more about the position and your team."},
    {heading:"Strong choice: warm professionalism shows adaptability.",impact:"You respect the interviewer’s pace while remaining confident, interested, and approachable.",coaching:"Use a calm greeting, attentive posture, and thoughtful answers. Allow rapport to develop instead of forcing it.",sample:"Thank you for the opportunity to speak with you. I reviewed the position carefully, and I am ready to discuss how my experience could support the team."},
    {heading:"A reserved style does not automatically mean rejection.",impact:"Withdrawing may create the very disconnection you fear and can make your answers sound uncertain or uninterested.",coaching:"Do not mind-read. Stay engaged, answer fully, and judge the interview from the actual questions and follow-up—not facial expression alone.",sample:"I appreciate the question. A relevant example from my last position is…, and the result was…"},
    {heading:"More volume is not the same as more connection.",impact:"Overpowering a reserved interviewer can look socially unaware and may make the conversation less comfortable.",coaching:"Keep positive energy, but slow your pace and match the room’s professional intensity.",sample:"I am very interested in the position. I will walk you through one example that shows how I could contribute."}
  ]},
  {id:"s6",question:"The interviewer asks whether you have questions.",choices:["Say no because asking questions could seem demanding.","Ask only how quickly you can be promoted.","Ask what success in the role would look like after the first several months.","Ask a question that was already answered clearly."],correct:2,feedback:[
    {heading:"Having no questions can look like low preparation or low interest.",impact:"The interviewer loses an opportunity to see your curiosity, judgment, and concern for doing the job well.",coaching:"Prepare at least three questions and choose one or two that were not already answered.",sample:"Yes, thank you. What would you want the person in this role to accomplish during the first 90 days?"},
    {heading:"Promotion-only questions make the conversation self-focused.",impact:"Ambition is not wrong, but asking only about advancement may suggest that the current role is merely a temporary stop.",coaching:"First ask about performance, priorities, training, or team needs. Ask about growth after showing commitment to succeeding in the role.",sample:"What does strong performance look like in this position, and what development opportunities become available after someone demonstrates that performance?"},
    {heading:"Strong choice: this question focuses on contribution.",impact:"It demonstrates preparation, future thinking, and interest in meeting the employer’s real expectations.",coaching:"Make it even stronger by using the employer’s timeline or priorities mentioned earlier in the interview.",sample:"Six months from now, what results would make you feel that hiring me was the right decision?"},
    {heading:"Repeating an answered question suggests weak listening.",impact:"Even a good prepared question loses value if the interviewer already addressed it clearly.",coaching:"Acknowledge what you heard and ask a thoughtful follow-up instead of repeating the original question.",sample:"You mentioned that the first month focuses on training. What tends to separate people who become productive quickly from those who struggle?"}
  ]}
];

